AB  - OBJECTIVE: User acceptance of digital health interventions in light to moderate depressive disorders has been rudimentarily researched and is examined in this study. METHODS: Problem-centered individual interviews were conducted with 3 affected persons, 3 relatives and 13 health care professionals. The data evaluation was based on the structured qualitative content analysis. RESULTS: Along the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology attitudes of affected persons, relatives and health care professionals were presented. Expectations of performance and the supportive framework conditions were the most significant influencing predictors for the positive intention to use. CONCLUSION: The results provide a basis for prioritizing needs and attitudes in next step. In terms of ethical, sustainable and economical use further research on user acceptance is required.
